1
00:00:00,526 --> 00:00:05,540
You might have come across news headlines like these quite frequently over the past few days.

2
00:00:06,094 --> 00:00:11,204
And looking at all of this, you might have wondered what's actually happening in the world of AI.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:18,640
Because clearly, AI is no longer just about transcribing audio, writing emails, answering questions, or generating images.

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,560
It's moving way beyond that.

5
00:00:20,782 --> 00:00:32,740
It's solving problems that have challenged researchers for years, proving complex mathematical theories, identifying serious software vulnerabilities, and even making discoveries that could accelerate scientific research.

6
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:37,380
And honestly, the pace at which all of this is happening is quite fascinating.

7
00:00:37,774 --> 00:00:46,212
So, in today's video, we are going to explore some extraordinary AI breakthroughs and understand what makes each one of them so significant.

8
00:00:46,542 --> 00:00:49,760
And don't worry if some of these headlines sound too technical.

9
00:00:49,920 --> 00:01:01,124
We are going to bring everything down into simple language, understand what's actually happening behind the scenes, and get an overview of how AI is evolving and what these developments could mean for our future.

10
00:01:01,582 --> 00:01:03,044
So let's get started.

11
00:01:03,598 --> 00:01:13,796
Starting with cybersecurity, OpenAI has introduced its new model, GPT 6 Cyber, specifically designed for advanced cybersecurity research.

12
00:01:14,222 --> 00:01:25,412
To demonstrate its capabilities, OpenAI reported that the model helped identify 81 previously unknown high severity vulnerabilities in Mozilla Firefox.

13
00:01:25,934 --> 00:01:38,788
It also developed 948 working exploit in the Linux kernel demonstrating how existing software vulnerabilities could potentially be used to gain unauthorized access or higher level system privileges.

14
00:01:39,342 --> 00:01:47,236
OpenAI says these findings were shared with the respective developers to help identify and fix the security flaws.

15
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:53,476
This makes a significant development in how AI models can contribute to cybersecurity research.

16
00:01:54,062 --> 00:01:58,884
Next, we have a major development from Google DeepMind in the field of genetics.

17
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:11,492
Google has launched Alpha Genome Atlas, an AI-powered platform that maps the potential effects of approximately 9 billion DNA changes across the human genome.

18
00:02:11,854 --> 00:02:20,836
Now, to understand the scale of this, our DNA contains around 3 billion positions, and even a single letter change can affect how certain genes function.

19
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:29,668
Alpha genome uses AI to predict how these changes could impact gene activity, RNA processing, and protein production.

20
00:02:29,934 --> 00:02:38,756
Google has also introduced an impact scoring system to help researchers identify which genetic changes deserve closer attention.

21
00:02:39,118 --> 00:02:46,852
This could significantly accelerate genetic research and help scientists investigate the underlying cause of certain diseases.

22
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:54,884
Moving on to mathematics, Anthropic has announced another major achievement by its AI model, Claude.

23
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:05,572
Claude has successfully formalized the proof of Fermat's last theorem, a famous mathematical problem that remained unsolved for more than 350 years.

24
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:17,200
The original theorem was proposed by French mathematician Piré de Fermat in 1637 and was eventually proved by mathematician Andrew Wills in 1995.

25
00:03:17,582 --> 00:03:26,400
Anthropic says Claude took that established proof and converted it into a form that a computer could check using a tool called Lean.

26
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:32,164
The project took about 11 days and produced around 13 million lines of lean code.

27
00:03:32,782 --> 00:03:38,000
Here's the distinction is that Claude did not invent the original proof or discover the theorem.

28
00:03:38,190 --> 00:03:44,260
Its achievement was translating a very complex human proof into a machine checkable version.

29
00:03:44,686 --> 00:03:50,756
That matters because checking mathematical proofs can take experts months or even years.

30
00:03:51,904 --> 00:04:01,104
Next, we have a development involving one of the most famous mathematical challenges, the Navier Stokes millennium price problem.

31
00:04:01,584 --> 00:04:13,668
Back in 2000, the Clay Mathematics Institute listed this as one of the seven major unsolved mathematical problems with a price of $1 million for a solution.

32
00:04:14,062 --> 00:04:26,788
For roughly 90 years, mathematicians have struggled with a deep question about whether smooth fluid can develop what mathematicians call a singularity, where the mathematical description breaks down.

33
00:04:27,184 --> 00:04:37,316
Now, OpenAI has targeted this long-standing problem and claims its AI research system has produced a mathematical proof addressing this question.

34
00:04:37,710 --> 00:04:49,252
What's particularly interesting is that around 10,000 AI agents reportedly work together on this research with the resulting proof formalized using lean for computer verification.

35
00:04:49,504 --> 00:04:57,264
If the result withstands independent mathematical scrutiny, it would mark a major milestone in mathematics.

36
00:04:57,646 --> 00:05:02,304
Now, let's look at something that could eventually influence drug discovery.

37
00:05:02,544 --> 00:05:07,844
Anthropics Clod has now demonstrated the ability to design protein binders.

38
00:05:08,558 --> 00:05:14,596
Small proteins that attach to specific target proteins and can potentially change how they function.

39
00:05:15,246 --> 00:05:34,116
What makes this development significant is that Claude didn't just generate designs on a computer, it autonomously studied target proteins, generated and ranked potential designs, and selected candidates for laboratory testing, which designs were then tested by external authorities.

40
00:05:34,670 --> 00:05:46,884
According to the reported results, 14 out of 15 targets had successful designs with 354 confirmed protein binders from 1320 designs tested.

41
00:05:47,438 --> 00:05:58,532
This is an important step towards using AI not just to understand biology but to actively design new biological molecules and potentially accelerate drug discovery.

42
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:02,864
Note that this is the only an early research component.

43
00:06:03,024 --> 00:06:07,844
It still needs substantial testing before it could become a safe and effective drug.

44
00:06:09,104 --> 00:06:16,580
Moving on, OpenAI has announced three major AI-generated results across mathematics, computer science, and physics.

45
00:06:17,102 --> 00:06:27,204
First, in mathematics, OpenAI claims its model proved a result about how waves focus with consequences for the Kakia and Stein conjectures.

46
00:06:27,662 --> 00:06:41,476
Second, in computer science, OpenAI says its system disproved the exponential time hypothesis, an assumption researchers have used for decades when estimating how difficult certain computing problems are.

47
00:06:41,902 --> 00:06:57,060
And third, in physics, the company reports mathematical existence proofs for certain quantum field theories, including work connected to quantum chromodynamics, which is the theory describing the strong force inside atomic nuclei.

48
00:06:57,454 --> 00:07:08,544
Now, a very important point these are OpenAI's research announcements, and the company itself says outside experts are being bought in to examine and explain the results.

49
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:15,044
So we should call them significant AI-generated research claims, not pretend every implication is already settled science.

50
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:19,780
Next, let's look at two interesting mathematical breakthroughs.

51
00:07:20,174 --> 00:07:27,076
First, the Aronson Amben's conjecture and open problem dating back to 2008.

52
00:07:27,424 --> 00:07:36,868
In simple terms, it asks whether a quantum computer can be exponentially faster than a normal computer when solving problems with random inputs.

53
00:07:37,264 --> 00:07:48,644
A company called Physical Superintelligence, using a non-public anthropic model says its AI research system proved the Aronson and Bannon's conjectures in roughly three days.

54
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:56,452
The second is the Careless conjecture, a 31-year-old mathematical problem proposed in 1995.

55
00:07:56,846 --> 00:08:03,556
It deals with how efficiently we can sample and understand high dimensional probability distributions.

56
00:08:04,078 --> 00:08:11,524
Microsoft researchers used AI assisted techniques to develop a solution, exploring more than 100 possible approaches.

57
00:08:11,918 --> 00:08:20,228
Both developments highlight how AI is increasingly helping researchers tackle mathematical problems that have remained open for decades.

58
00:08:21,358 --> 00:08:29,572
Next, we have Spark Matter, an AI-powered research assistant designed to accelerate the discovery of new materials.

59
00:08:29,984 --> 00:08:35,300
Finding new materials with specific properties is a time-consuming research process.

60
00:08:35,854 --> 00:08:41,504
Spark Matter aims to simplify this using a team of AI agents that work together.

61
00:08:41,966 --> 00:08:53,092
It generates material ideas, plans and simulates experiments, evaluates the results, filters out weaker candidates, and continuously refines its approach.

62
00:08:53,344 --> 00:09:01,924
The goal is to identify promising new materials much faster and reduce the amount of manual experimentation required.

63
00:09:02,254 --> 00:09:11,012
Essentially, AI is now moving beyond analyzing existing scientific knowledge to actively assisting researchers in discovering something new.

64
00:09:12,046 --> 00:09:31,972
So, these were some of the most extraordinary AI development and breakthroughs, making headlines right now, from identifying cybersecurity vulnerabilities and verifying decades old mathematical proofs to exploring unsolved scientific problems, mapping billions of DNA changes, and even designing new proteins and materials.

65
00:09:32,304 --> 00:09:35,364
AI is moving far beyond what we once imagined.

66
00:09:35,664 --> 00:09:41,444
And what's truly fascinating is that AI is no longer just assisting humans with everyday tasks.

67
00:09:41,904 --> 00:09:48,996
It's increasingly becoming a powerful tool for research, discovery, and solving problems that have challenged experts for decades.

68
